A two-way prop the call site leaves unbound is a cell only where the target can
write it. `ui.table`'s `selected` is reported by bubbletea's table widget, whose
`binds` descriptor names it, so there each table keeps a cell. html's table is
markup with no way to report a selection, so a table under a const `for` stays
plain markup there: no cell, no instance built at run time, and the loop unrolls
into static `<table>`s. `table_cell_kept_bubbletea.txtar` is the other half.

Before, every unbound `selected` was a cell, so a table under a `for` was a
component with state, built at run time by a factory -- which is how the docs
site's per-target option tables became JavaScript.

deny out/none/html/index.html `function __table_state` -- html's table holds no cell, so no factory builds it
deny out/none/html/index.html `<table id=` -- a static table needs no id for a script to find it

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    none { html }
}

const groups = [["a", "b"], ["c"]]

ui.window(title="tables") {
    ui.vbox {
        for var g = groups {
            ui.table(columns=["name"], rows=[g])
        }
    }
}
